In the discussion, James presents a model of greenhouse warming using concentric circles to illustrate the balance of energy in and out. He initially struggles with the assumption that upward and downward radiation from the atmosphere must be equal, questioning the incorporation of greenhouse gas (GHG) effects. I clarify that in the classic one-layer greenhouse model, the downwelling radiation constitutes recycled surface energy rather than an external input. To achieve higher temperatures, I suggest using multiple atmospheric layers or a continuous optical depth, explaining how an increase in layers effectively doubles the downward radiation at the surface. Each layer’s emission is balanced by increased absorption from below, ultimately leading to a scenario akin to Venus with significantly higher surface temperatures due to thick, GHG-rich atmospheres.
